    "result_text": "There is a single test class, and it passes along with the full build.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec requires validating the E.164 national-number length against the country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting mismatches with a 400.\n\nI made one small, self-contained change in `E164.java`:\n\n- Added a private static helper `requireNationalLength(String)` that derives the national digit count by stripping the recognized country code (`61` or `1`) and throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (already mapped to HTTP 400) when it doesn't match the required length. Unknown country codes are left unchecked.\n- `normalize` gained a single line \u2014 a call to the new helper \u2014 so its own complexity is unchanged.\n\nThis keeps structural impact low: the new behaviour lives in a cohesive new unit rather than growing `normalize`, only one file is touched, and the existing `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 400 handler is reused. The new test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` passes, and the full build is green.\n\nNote: `+61 123` was already rejected by the existing 8\u201315 total-digit rule, but the new per-country check also correctly catches the subtler cases (e.g. `+61 12345678` \u2014 10 total digits, but only 8 national) that previously slipped through.",
